# app/.dev.vars.example — the secrets the WORKER reads at runtime.
#
# HOW THIS WORKS
#   • This file is committed and carries KEY NAMES ONLY, never a value. It is
#     the reviewable list of what the Worker expects, and `secrets:check`
#     compares it against what each Worker actually holds.
#   • It sits beside app/wrangler.jsonc, because the secrets script reads
#     `.dev.vars` from the folder that holds the wrangler config. Copy it to a
#     git-ignored `.dev.vars` in this same folder and fill in the real values:
#         cp app/.dev.vars.example app/.dev.vars
#   • Load the production Worker from it — one command, no `wrangler secret put`
#     to forget:
#         npm run secrets:push
#   • In a linked worktree, app/.dev.vars is the branch's own copy. Adds and
#     rotations also go to the primary worktree's copy now; deletions wait for
#     the merge. See wiki/development/secrets.md.
#
# THIS IS NOT THE ROOT `.env`
#   The root `.env` holds what you and the scripts use to reach Cloudflare,
#   chiefly CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN. It must NEVER reach a Worker, and
#   `secrets:push` refuses to load it. Which file holds what:
#   wiki/stack/d1-pipeline.md#env-and-devvars-are-not-interchangeable
#
# STAGING VALUES
#   Previews run unreviewed branch code, so they never get these values. The
#   staging Worker loads only a git-ignored `app/.dev.vars.staging`, holding test
#   keys: a payment test key, a sandbox inbox. With no such file, previews hold
#   no keys. `secrets:push` refuses a staging file that repeats a live value or
#   names a live-app-only key. See wiki/stack/staging-bindings.md.

# --- Example entries (delete these; replace with your Worker's real secrets) ---

# Secret key for the payments provider. Use the TEST key in .dev.vars.staging.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# Where the app posts outbound webhooks. Point staging at a sink, not at prod.
# WEBHOOK_URL=

# The live app's key for its own sign-in list. Setup makes it and stores it in
# the production Worker for you: leave it out of this file. `secrets:push`
# refuses a staging file that names it. See wiki/stack/employee-access.md.
# WONG_ACCESS_LOGIN_MANAGEMENT=

# The read-only Cloudflare key behind Cloudflare look-ups. Setup makes it and
# stores it in the production Worker for you: leave it out of this file. `secrets:push`
# refuses any source that names it. See wiki/stack/cloudflare-credentials.md.
# WONG_CLOUDFLARE_READ=

# --- The project itself ---

# A GitHub fine-grained token for this one repository, with five permissions and nothing else: Contents:
# Read and write; Pull requests: Read and write; Actions: Read-only; Commit statuses: Read-only;
# Deployments: Read-only. It carries no Workflows permission, so a saved change can not rewrite the checks.
# Connect your assistant uses it to hand the project to people given Project code in Access, and to save
# their changes. Only the live app holds it. A project kept in Cloudflare needs none: leave it blank there. See
# wiki/stack/employee-project.md.
WONG_CODE_WRITE=

# The read-only key WONG_CODE_WRITE replaces: Contents: Read-only and nothing else. An install that already
# holds it keeps handing the project out, and saves nothing until the key above is in. A new install leaves
# it blank. See wiki/stack/employee-project.md.
WONG_CODE_READ=
